uld  like the device to do ideally  but now especially in a cryptographic  setting where  you might want to use this device to  generate hopefully secure keys  you might start to wonder whether the  device really does  inside what you ask it to do or whether  perhaps it does something entirely  different that would mean that  your secure keys for example aren't  secure after all  and this is where device independence  comes in  so the idea in device independence is  that from  purely classical interaction with this  black box device  we obtain some desired output so this  might be random numbers the answer to  some computational  problem or secure keys or whatnot  but at the same time we also certify  that the device behaved as  intended so therefore we no longer need  to place any  trust in the device itself we certify  that the device  does what we hope it would do and  therefore we can also use devices that  have been prepared by a potentially  malicious  adversary so if we just  look at this setting this might seem  somewhat impossible because you know  just you  down here potentially much more powerful  black box device up here  how can you possibly constrain the  actions that this device can take  and indeed we need to make some minimal  assumption about this device  and the standard assumption that is  being made  is that this isn't one big monolithic  device but instead it consists of two  components or sub-devices  and these two components here they may  be entangled  but crucially they cannot communicate  with each other so this is why i'll call  this the non-communication assumption  you as the classical verifier still get  to classically interact with each  component individually  these two components just can't talk to  each other  so now let's see what self-testing is  in this standard setting so for  self-testing  we basically need to describe what the  protocol is that the verify  executes here and the protocol is very  simple the verify just sends  