s is that  you know this component here could for  example just apply some unitary or some  isometry  to both its state and its measurements  and this would leave  the classical measurement outcome that  the verifier gets to see  the same so therefore we can never hope  for the verifier  to somehow constrain the basis that each  component chooses here  and maybe just for addition when we say  a spade is  for example an epr pair up to a local  change of faces  what you should think of operationally  is that  we could have extracted an epr pair from  this state  just using local operations on each  component  so this is the standard self-testing  definition and as i said in in this talk  we're interested in  changing this setup here so in  particular we want to get rid of this  non-communication assumption  so the setting that we consider instead  is one where  we just have one big monolithic black  box  device that the verifier still only gets  to interact with  classically but we make a new assumption  this computational assumption so  specifically as i already said earlier  we assume  that this device is unable to break  post-quantum crypto  and more specifically we assume that it  is unable to break the learning with  errors problem  during the time it takes to execute the  protocol  so always denote computationally bounded  devices with these little gears  so you should be able to see the  difference  all right so now let's try to take our  self  testing definition from the standard  settings so i've just copied here what  we had  two slides ago let's try to translate  this to the single device setting  so for concreteness in the single device  setting you should just think of these  questions x and y here as being sent  together  in one go to the device and then the  verifier receiving the two answers  in one go as well as we'll see in a  minute the actual protocol is slightly  more complicated and has more rounds of  interaction  but you can just think of this 