es that we also use in our work  were introduced  in two previous papers the first is on  certifiable information theoretic  randomness expansion by professionalism  and the second is this well-known  verification protocol for quantum  computations due to mahada  and just because this is a confusion  that comes up  um occasionally i want to briefly  clarify the difference between  verification and self-testing  what suffices to show for verification  and this is what  mahadev's protocol shows is that the  verifier can certify that there exists  a low energy eigenstate of some local  hamiltonian  so of course you could pick the epr  hamiltonian to be this local hamiltonian  so this is this hamiltonian and the low  energy eigenstate  the unique ground state of this local  hamiltonian is the epr pair  but now you're just certifying that the  epr pair exists in a mathematical sense  and and this isn't interesting right  rolling knew this all along  so what self-testing certifies is the  strongest statement that the device has  actually prepared this epr pair and then  measured it  up to this change of basis so the  difference between verification  and self-testing is that verification  just certifies existence of the state  whereas self-testing certifies that the  device is actually prepared and measure  the state  so the last piece of previous work that  i want to mention is this remote state  preparation protocol by yorkie and vidic  and this you can think of as single  qubit  self-testing so i want to explain this  in a little more detail because we can  use this as a basis for explaining how  our self-testing protocol vpr path works  so roughly how does this protocol go  we start by fixing some collection of  single keyboard states so for example  let's just pick these four states zero  and one are the computational basis  states and plus and minus are the  automatic basis states  and then the protocol has some initial  setup rounds that involve some  clever use of 