d thing that we need to do is  that we don't just want to prepare a two  qubit product state but we want to  prepare an epr state  in a tangled state so the device should  entangle  its qubits with respect to the tensor  product structure that the verifier  enforced in this first step here so  this first step is in many ways the  technically  difficult one but we'll mostly take this  for granted and i'll explain how the  second step works simply because it's  quite hard to give intuition for how  this first one goes  so now let's just build a self-testing  protocol  for epr states um for this we can start  out with  two copies of this rrsp protocol and  if we were to execute these two copies  on separate  isolated devices then just by the  guarantee from the rsp protocol  we know that each of these devices here  must have prepared one of these for  single keyword states  so now let's just execute two parallel  copies  of the rsp protocol on one big device  and now what we need to show and this is  this enforcing of the tensor product  structure that i said i wasn't going to  explain in a lot of detail  we can show that this one big device  now needs to prepare these two qubits  individually so it essentially needs to  treat the two copies of the rsp protocol  separately  inside and prepare one qubit for each  copy of the protocol  this kind of has the flavor of parallel  repetition or direct product theorems  so if we take it for granted that we can  buy two parallel copies of the rsp  protocol  generate these product states here  how can we entangle them and for this  it's useful to rewrite these four by  four states  like this so this means these are the  four states 0 0  0 1 1 0 and 1 1 and and likewise down  here  and now we need to entangle them so  let's just ask the device  very kindly to execute a c not gate on  its two prepared qubits here  of course we need to certify that the  device has applied the c naught gate  correctly  but let's just consider what would  happen if we were to apply  a c not gate on these states and you can  check  just by straightforward calculation that  these first three families of states  here  they essentially remain invariant under  the c naught  so for example one zero is mapped to one  one  which is a different state but it's the  same kind of state  in contrast these two qubit product  states down here  are mapped to the four bell pairs by the  c naught gate  so the epr state would be zero zero plus  1  1 but in fact we get all four bell pairs  so why is this a useful observation it's  a useful observation  because this means that in these first  three kinds of cases here all the c  naught does  is just a re-labeling so  then if we also appropriately relabel  the checks that the verifier  did in the two copies of the rsp  protocol without a c not gate  then we can still certify single keyword  measurements  as before as when we didn't apply the c  not gate  when we didn't ask the device to apply  the c naught gate  and in this last case down here we can  now  certify bell-like correlations so this  means we can certify basically that the  parity  of these two bits here is is the  expected one  and now the last step that we need to do  is we need to somehow  combine these two statements so we need  to carry over this single qubit  measurement certification  to the bell case down here  and for this we rely on this blindness  property that i explained before  so remember that blindness basically  says that the device  cannot distinguish which of these four  cases  it is in and therefore intuitively it  also  can't change its behavior based on which  case  it is and what this allows us to do  is that we can carry over the certified  behavior in these first three kinds of  cases  down to this last case as well and in  total what that gets us is that we can  certify single keyboard measurements on  bell states  and this is the self-testing statement  that we wanted  okay so i hope this gives