d thing that we need to do is  that we don't just want to prepare a two  qubit product state but we want to  prepare an epr state  in a tangled state so the device should  entangle  its qubits with respect to the tensor  product structure that the verifier  enforced in this first step here so  this first step is in many ways the  technically  difficult one but we'll mostly take this  for granted and i'll explain how the  second step works simply because it's  quite hard to give intuition for how  this first one goes  so now let's just build a self-testing  protocol  for epr states um for this we can start  out with  two copies of this rrsp protocol and  if we were to execute these two copies  on separate  isolated devices then just by the  guarantee from the rsp protocol  we know that each of these devices here  must have prepared one of these for  single keyword states  so now let's just execute two parallel  copies  of the rsp protocol on one big device  and now what we need to show and this is  this enforcing of the tensor product  structure that i said i wasn't going to  explain in a lot of detail  we can show that this one big device  now needs to prepare these two qubits  individually so it essentially needs to  treat the two copies of the rsp protocol  separately  inside and prepare one qubit for each  copy of the protocol  this kind of has the flavor of parallel  repetition or direct product theorems  so if we take it for granted that we can  buy two parallel copies of the rsp  protocol  generate these product states here  how can we entangle them and for this  it's useful to rewrite these four by  four states  like this so this means these are the  four states 0 0  0 1 1 0 and 1 1 and and likewise down  here  and now we need to entangle them so  let's just ask the device  very kindly to execute a c not gate on  its two prepared qubits here  of course we need to certify that the  device has applied the c naught gate  correctly  but let's just consider what would  happen if we were to apply  a c not gate on these states and you can  check  just by straightforward calculation that  these first three families of states  here  they essentially remain invariant under  the c naught  so for example one zero is mapped to one  one  which is a different state but it's the  same kind of state  in contrast these two qubit product  states down here  are mapped to the four bell pairs by the  c naught gate  so the epr state would be zero zero plus  1  1 but in fact we get all four bell pairs  so why is this a useful observation it's  a useful observation  because this means that in these first  three kinds of cases here all the c  naught does  is just a re-labeling so  then if we also appropriately relabel  the checks that the verifier  did in the two copies of the rsp  protocol without a c not gate  then we can still certify single keyword  measurements  as before as when we didn't apply the c  not gate  when we didn't ask the device to apply  the c naught gate  and in this last case down here we can  now  certify bell-like correlations so this  means we can certify basically that the  parity  of these two bits here is is the  expected one  and now the last step that we need to do  is we need to somehow  combine these two statements so we need  to carry over this single qubit  measurement certification  to the bell case down here  and for this we rely on this blindness  property that i explained before  so remember that blindness basically  says that the device  cannot distinguish which of these four  cases  it is in and therefore intuitively it  also  can't change its behavior based on which  case  it is and what this allows us to do  is that we can carry over the certified  behavior in these first three kinds of  cases  down to this last case as well and in  total what that gets us is that we can  certify single keyboard measurements on  bell states  and this is the self-testing statement  that we wanted  okay so i hope this gives you some  intuition for how the protocol  goes so let me wrap up the main result  that i  showed is that we can construct a  self-testing protocol that  does away with this non-communication  assumption that  most self-testing protocols relied on  previously  and instead we can base the security  of the protocol on the device not being  able to break post quantum cryptography  and the main technique that we build on  in our work is this tight cryptographic  leash for black box quantum devices that  was introduced by berkrisk and others  so in terms of future work and if we  actually want to  implement our self-testing protocol in  practice someday  then for that we would have to improve  the robustness balance so  robustness refers to the fact that if  the device  wins with probability one minus epsilon  then how close as a function of epsilon  must the device's actual strategy be to  the ideal one or the desired one  and we do show that our protocol is  robust  in this sense it's just that the  robustness found is quite poor  numerically  secondly and perhaps more interestingly  from a theoretical point of view is  whether we can  self-test for arbitrary states in this  manner  so in our protocol we can self-test for  epr states and in fact we can self-test  for  any of the four bell pairs but of course  we would like to extend this to a larger  class of states  and i think there are two ways one could  try to go about this the first  is to take our protocol and the  techniques there and just  try to extend them to work for a larger  class of states  and the second one would be to try to  start with  mahadas certification protocol and  somehow boost  that guarantee from certifying the  existence  of ground states of local hamiltonians  to actually  certifying that the device has prepared  this ground state and then measured it  and in this latter direction there has  been some work under the  banner of quantum proofs of knowledge  but even that  seems to be a somewhat weaker statement  still than  self-testing and finally this  cryptographic leash that we've used in  our work  has potentially many more applications  so as one example of this and recently  joint work with retirement friedman  under accolade and if you do like  we were able to show that based on this  self-testing protocol we can also get a  protocol for device independent quantum  feed distribution  that similarly does away with a  non-communication assumption  and i think there are potentially many  more such applications  all right so i will stop here and thank  you very much for your attention
 

